  "knowledge": {
    "progressSummary": "COMPLETED and verified in main. Proofs/CauchySchwarzOQ02OQ04.lean (201 lines, 11 theorems, 0 sorries, 0 axioms, no native_decide) formalizes the operator-norm Cauchy–Schwarz inequality ‖⟪T x, y⟫‖ ≤ ‖T‖·‖x‖·‖y‖ over any RCLike field, the numerical-radius bound w(T) ≤ ‖T‖, submultiplicativity under composition and scaling, the adjoint form, recovery of classical Cauchy–Schwarz from the identity, and the positive-operator (Kadison–Schwarz) inequality with its square-root form via a discriminant argument. Gallery entry present (meta.json + annotations.json), badge 'original', status 'verified'; depends only on standard Mathlib operator/inner-product lemmas over the foundational axioms.",
    "builtItems": [
      "operator_cauchy_schwarz: ‖⟪T x, y⟫‖ ≤ ‖T‖·‖x‖·‖y‖ for a bounded operator T over any RCLike field (substitute ‖T x‖ ≤ ‖T‖·‖x‖ into scalar Cauchy–Schwarz).",
      "operator_numerical_radius_bound: ‖⟪T x, x⟫‖ ≤ ‖T‖·‖x‖² — numerical radius dominated by operator norm (y = x specialization).",
      "operator_cs_comp / operator_cs_smul: submultiplicativity of the bound under operator composition and scalar multiplication.",
      "operator_cs_adjoint: adjoint form ‖⟪T† y, x⟫‖ ≤ ‖T‖·‖y‖·‖x‖ via ⟪T† y, x⟫ = ⟪y, T x⟫; recovers_classical_cs: identity operator recovers scalar ‖⟪x, y⟫‖ ≤ ‖x‖·‖y‖.",
      "positive_operator_cauchy_schwarz + positive_operator_cs_abs: Kadison–Schwarz ⟪T x, y⟫² ≤ ⟪T x, x⟫·⟪T y, y⟫ for positive symmetric T (discriminant of a nonnegative quadratic), and the √ form.",
      "Gallery entry src/data/proofs/cauchy-schwarz-oq-02-oq-04 (meta.json + annotations.json)."
    ],
    "insights": [
      "Operator-norm Cauchy–Schwarz is one substitution from the scalar case: replace ‖T x‖ by its defining bound ‖T‖·‖x‖.",
      "The numerical radius w(T) = sup over unit x of ‖⟪T x, x⟫‖ is dominated by the operator norm — the y = x specialization.",
      "A positive symmetric operator turns ⟪T x, y⟫ into a semi-inner product, so it satisfies its own (commutative Kadison–Schwarz) Cauchy–Schwarz inequality.",
      "The discriminant trick: a quadratic in t nonnegative everywhere has b² − 4ac ≤ 0, which IS Cauchy–Schwarz for the form ⟨·,·⟩_T; composition (‖S T‖ ≤ ‖S‖·‖T‖) and scaling, with ‖id‖ ≤ 1, recover the classical case."
    ],
